            'faqs' => [
                ['Is it true I need no qualifications at all?', '<p>That is what Qualifi publishes. The entry section for this award consists of one sentence stating that it is an open entry qualification, with the only guidance being that candidates have IELTS 5.5 or other evidence of English competence at that level. No prior certificate and no minimum age appear. Individual centres may still apply their own admission checks, so ask yours what it requires in practice.</p>', 1],
                ['What English will I actually need to show?', '<p>Qualifi points to IELTS 5.5 or other evidence of competence at an equivalent standard, which leaves room for alternatives such as a recognised school qualification taught in English or another accepted test. It is phrased as guidance rather than as a hard threshold. Your centre will decide what it accepts, so send your evidence over early and have it confirmed before you pay anything.</p>', 2],
                ['Why does it carry 120 credits for only four subjects?', '<p>That ratio is higher than on Qualifi\'s 60-credit awards, which list four to six subjects. The qualification page does not break credits down subject by subject, so we cannot tell you how they are distributed. Qualifi publishes a combined specification covering several of its hospitality and tourism diplomas, and that document is where the breakdown lives. We have flagged this rather than guessing at numbers.</p>', 3],
                ['How does it differ from the Level 5 extended diploma in the same field?', '<p>By level and by depth. This award introduces the two industries and the commercial basics; Qualifi\'s Level 5 extended diploma in hospitality and tourism management sits two levels higher and assumes you already have grounding. Level 3 is comparable to school-leaving standard in England, so this is where you begin rather than where you specialise.</p>', 4],
                ['Does it qualify me to manage a hotel?', '<p>It introduces management principles within the sector; it does not appoint you to anything. The title contains the word management because the subject matter is managerial, not because the certificate confers a role. Employers decide who manages their operations, and they generally want experience alongside a qualification. Treat this as the knowledge base that makes the experience count for more.</p>', 5],
                ['Who sets the start dates and the price?', '<p>The approved centre, every time. Qualifi writes and regulates the qualification and issues your certificate, but it does not teach, does not run intakes and publishes no fee for any of its awards. Anything about scheduling, delivery format, assessment dates and total cost should be put to the centre, preferably in writing before you enrol.</p>', 6],
            ],
